{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "martin-2-tile-servers-2026-09",
  "slug": "martin-2-tile-servers",
  "category": "Tile servers",
  "title": "Martin 2.0 vs 1.16.1 and nine competitors",
  "deck": "Martin 2.0 isn't out yet, so I built the release PR and ran it against 1.16.1 and the newest stable release of nine servers it competes with, in both of Martin's jobs: handing tiles out of an archive and cutting them from PostGIS. Everything the September pages measured, plus CPU per tile, capacity under a latency SLO, a busy map, and a thousand sources. I've committed code to Martin. The runner's public; check my numbers.",
  "unit": "ms",
  "lowerIsBetter": true,
  "verdict": {
    "winnerId": "martin-2-pmtiles",
    "headline": "Martin 2.0 is the fastest archive server here by a factor of three and carries a busy PostGIS map in two-thirds of anyone else's time; it won 8 of 22 categories handily and gave the rest up on startup, memory, single tiles and the database",
    "summary": "Serving PMTiles, 2.0 is the release to wait for: 88,017 tiles a second on the random walk to 1.16.1's 21,245, on 28 µs of CPU a tile instead of 188, and 96,000 tiles a second inside a 50 ms p99 where 1.16.1 gave out at 8,000. Most of that is one change: local PMTiles files are read in place now instead of through the object-store client, which also took 1.16.1's 150 MB of heap after load down to single digits. MBTiles got about 1.5× faster. From PostGIS the database does 98% of the work, so 2.0 ties 1.16.1 on throughput and leads the field by 5 to 8% where it leads at all; BBOX edges it at ten clients. 2.0 also went backwards in three PostGIS categories: single tiles on light layers, one ladder step of capacity, and startup with a thousand tables. All three trace to 2.0 typing every table's columns at startup, which costs a query per table and leaves extra pooled connections for light requests to rotate through. VersaTiles starts in 65 ms to Martin's 76 and holds 2.4 MiB to its 10.6; go-pmtiles opens a thousand archives in 96 ms by not opening them; pg_tileserv does the same with a thousand tables. None of that is a reason to skip 2.0. It's the to-do list."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "One machine: an M2 Max running macOS, with every server in an OrbStack Linux VM (12 CPUs) and each server container held to 4 CPUs and 4 GB. The load generator shares that VM. Ratios on x86 or Graviton Linux servers will differ.",
    "Martin 2.0.0 isn't released. It is a local build of the release PR at 055d4eaa with the release recipe, because the PR's own CI binaries are debug builds; a local 1.16.1 built the same way matched the official 1.16.1 image. If the published 2.0.0 image differs, this page gets re-run before it goes up.",
    "Martin 2.0.0 on PMTiles ran into the load generator's ceiling in the throughput tests (it used about 2.5 of its 4 cores), so its tiles per second are floors. CPU per tile is the fair comparison there.",
    "PostGIS numbers are mostly PostgreSQL: an eight-connection pool (BBOX hard-codes eight) and a 6-CPU database. They rank the SQL each server sends and how it queues, not the server's own code.",
    "Berlin only. A planet archive has deeper PMTiles directories and colder caches, and every tile here was served from the page cache.",
    "Tile caches are off everywhere, including Martin's in-memory cache, which is on by default. A deployment with it on serves repeated tiles faster than anything measured here.",
    "The map session flies a fixed path. About 42% of its archive requests fall outside the Berlin extract at low zoom and come back empty: 204 from most servers, 404 from VersaTiles, 500 from BBOX. Time counts for all of them; the status codes are on record, not scored.",
    "Behaviors are recorded, not ranked: the encoding each server picks for a browser's Accept-Encoding (VersaTiles recompresses to brotli, Martin gzips), what it sends a client that accepts no compression, and 204 against 404 for an empty tile.",
    "Memory ranks anonymous memory, the heap a process holds. Mmapped archive pages and the executable are file-backed and on record in the run file.",
    "Absolute numbers don't line up with the September pages: the load generator moved onto the Docker network, because the host's port forwarder capped a 242 KB tile at about 13,800 a second."
  ],
  "trademarks": [
    "Martin, Tegola, BBOX, pg_tileserv, TiPg, ldproxy, go-pmtiles, VersaTiles, mbtileserver and tileserver-gl are shown by name and brand colour only; none of these projects publishes a trademark policy that licenses its mark for third-party use."
  ],
